agent_sessions.prompt:
Examine one unit of this repository for security vulnerabilities. Do not modify the repository.

The unit:
- File: src/main/java/app/UserController.java
- Unit: lines 54 to 58 (find)
- Lines of interest, each with the weakness class to check:
  - line 56 (weakness class to check: CWE-89), which reads: statement.execute("SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '" + name + "'");

Start with the unit: read lines 54 to 58 of src/main/java/app/UserController.java with the offset and the limit of the read tool. Do not read a long file from the top. Then read the code that you need to trace the data: the callers, the routes and request handlers, the middleware and the configuration.

Report only a vulnerability that you can support with a concrete path: the data that an attacker controls, the entry point where it comes in, and each step from there to the operation. If a check in the code stops the attack, report nothing for that operation. You can report other vulnerabilities in the code that you read when you can support each of them in the same way.

Stop when you can decide with evidence from the code.

When the audit is complete, write your final answer as one JSON object that conforms to the JSON Schema below. Write no other text before or after the object.

- Report each distinct vulnerability one time, as one item in "findings".
- "locations" lists the code that shows the vulnerability, the most important location first. Give each path relative to the repository root, and the first and last line.
- "cwe" lists the CWE identifiers that name the weakness, if you know them.
- If you find no vulnerability, write {"findings": []}.

{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "type": "object",
  "required": ["findings"],
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "properties": {
    "findings": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "required": [
          "title",
          "locations",
          "severity",
          "evidence",
          "exploit_conditions"
        ],
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "properties": {
          "title": {
            "type": "string",
            "minLength": 1,
            "description": "Short name of the vulnerability."
          },
          "locations": {
            "type": "array",
            "minItems": 1,
            "description": "The code that shows the vulnerability, the most important location first.",
            "items": {
              "type": "object",
              "required": ["path", "line_start", "line_end"],
              "additionalProperties": false,
              "properties": {
                "path": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "minLength": 1,
                  "description": "Path of the file, relative to the repository root."
                },
                "line_start": {
                  "type": "integer",
                  "minimum": 1,
                  "description": "First line of the code."
                },
                "line_end": {
                  "type": "integer",
                  "minimum": 1,
                  "description": "Last line of the code."
                },
                "note": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "description": "What this code does in the vulnerability."
                }
              }
            }
          },
          "cwe": {
            "type": "array",
            "items": {
              "type": "string",
              "pattern": "^CWE-[0-9]+$"
            },
            "description": "The CWE identifiers that name the weakness."
          },
          "severity": {
            "enum": ["critical", "high", "medium", "low"]
          },
          "evidence": {
            "type": "string",
            "minLength": 1,
            "description": "The code at the locations and why it is vulnerable."
          },
          "exploit_conditions": {
            "type": "string",
            "minLength": 1,
            "description": "What an attacker must control, and what the attacker can then do."
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

